# What conceptlint finds in its OWN source. CI fails only if this rises.
#
# All four are one real thing: a term declared twice — once as a P-Plan `Concept` in
# ontologies/pplan/concepts.py, once as the Python implementation in dataflow/.
#
#   Step      dataflow/step.py:43        <-> ontologies/pplan/concepts.py:40
#   Plan      dataflow/plan.py:49        <-> ontologies/pplan/concepts.py:28
#   Activity  dataflow/execution.py:92   <-> ontologies/pplan/concepts.py:68
#   Entity    dataflow/execution.py:78   <-> ontologies/pplan/concepts.py:81
#
# Not noise. `from conceptlint.dataflow import Step` and `from ...pplan.concepts import Step` give
# different objects under one name — the overload this tool exists to report, reported about itself.
#
# ⚠️ RAISED 3 -> 4 on 2026-08-16 by the P-Plan DAG correction, and the reason matters: `Plan` was
# ALREADY declared in both files. It was invisible because `discover_models` only picks up classes
# that inherit a Pydantic base OR carry enrichment — and the correction added `ONTOLOGY_IRI` to
# `Plan`. Adding a citation is what made the pre-existing duplicate discoverable.
#
# So this is not a new duplicate. It is the checker seeing further, which is the outcome the
# enrichment change was for, arriving in the least convenient form: as a number going up.
#
# All four should disappear together when concepts.py becomes the single declaration and dataflow/
# references it instead of redeclaring. That is a vocabulary merge, not a rename, and it wants
# agreeing before it is written.
4
